		<div class="post" id="post-1468456">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,1468456)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-1468456">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/eskobar" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1173523); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1173523&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607958913" alt="Eskobar" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1173523)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/eskobar" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1173523); return false;" >Eskobar</a></span> <span class="odate time_1338649168 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">02 Jun 2012 14:59</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1468456">
									

<p>Erm. I like the sentiment, and feel-good stories about the Foundation in general (just because there are so few that highlight the personal side of the Foundation as just another job). But I really do have a standing policy against rape-as-drama, and the story just doesn't justify it well enough for me to overlook it. This is abandonment-as-drama, cancer-as-drama, <em>and</em> rape-as-drama.</p>
<p>Also, I maintain that I'm not sure this is how the Foundation hires people. She apparently has, what, a bachelor's in Biology? And the Foundation comes to <em>her</em>, offering her a job? When they could hire people with Masters and doctorates who had proven themselves as being competent in their fields?</p>
<p>And how is her mother &quot;elderly&quot; when she was 15?</p>
<p>And if she's 34, and she's worked for the Foundation for five years, did she only get her bachelor's when she was 29? What had she been doing before?</p>

							</div>

								
