    <div class="post-container" id="fpc-1468583">
                      
		<div class="post" id="post-1468583">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,1468583)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-1468583">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/adam-smascher" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(587467); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=587467&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607958913" alt="Adam Smascher" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=587467)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/adam-smascher" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(587467); return false;" >Adam Smascher</a></span> <span class="odate time_1338664109 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">02 Jun 2012 19:08</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1468583">
									

<p>This has a lot of really poor word choice and awkward phrasing. Below are a few examples, in a collapsible because long:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Show&nbsp;long</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;long</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;Daddy?&quot; The 5 year old Amy stared aimlessly at the wooden door, while her mother broke down into sobbing beside her.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Aimlessly isn't the word you want. Aimless implies a lack of direction, focus, or purpose. I think you meant blankly. I'm not sure, though, because that's a really strange choice. &quot;Broke down into sobbing,&quot; is wordier than it needs to be. One generally breaks down sobbing, or maybe even breaks into sobbing, but using both makes it read strangely.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>She watched as the new researcher to the biohazardous research team steadily cut away at the tumor that was once D-61293's stomach. She smiled as the new guy lifted the separated tumor from its growth place and placed it in a vat of formalin.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You started two sentences back-to-back with the same word, and in both, stated the researcher was new in both. You could substitute this and convey the same idea with fewer words, and brevity is wit:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>She watched as the new researcher to the biohazardous research team steadily cut away at the tumor that was once D-61293's stomach, smiling as he placed it in a vat of formalin.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is still weird because you tell us they cut out this guy's entire stomach, and yet, she's happy to be helping. Sweet Jesus do I not want her help.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;Excellent work, Rikard. Sew up the incision in the stomach wall, and let's patch 'im up and call it a day.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Oh, I suppose they didn't cut out his entire stomach after all, just excised a tumor. That's a relief, but why are you lying to me, Xiao?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>She shut the lid on the vat, and placed the unit on a conveyor belt that would take it to the pathologists working two blocks away.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You could simply say she closed the vat. The comma in the first sentence is entirely unnecessary. Your use of &quot;blocks&quot; is somewhat confusing: we've got this vat containing a biohazard of some description passing&#8230; underground, beneath a city, over a distance of two city blocks to the lab? This is piss poor site design and frankly insane handling of biohazardous material. If you mean that biohazardous materials must simply pass through two different wings of the site before reaching the lab, this is <em>also</em> piss poor site design, and still insane handling of the material.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>She took enjoyment from treating people of their pain, even if she injected them with the organism that she would have to take out of them eventually.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That first clause, &quot;She took enjoyment from treating people of their pain,&quot; is needlessly wordy. &quot;She enjoyed treating people's pain,&quot; or &quot;She took pleasure in relieving people's pain.&quot; Second clause of that sentence, you could axe everything after organism and not lose anything, or just say, &quot;even if she was the cause.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Amy couldn't hear herself crying over the flat, piercing tone of the ECG.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>They are not this loud. Eskobar already touched on the issues with an elderly woman having a 15 year old daughter, and the chronology issues and plot holes in general, so I won't get into those things. I'm not entirely sure that they'd wire her up with an ECG if she's on her deathbed and all treatment is merely palliative at this point, which is how you make it sound.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>All afternoon, she continued to click away at her computer, trying to put together DNA sequences for some of the superviruses that the Foundation has recently acquired.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Your tenses conflict. This is solved by simply removing &quot;has&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>She always wondered where the Foundation got such nasty ailments, but then thought to herself that she wasn't ready for the answer, and dropped that line of thought.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Every single time. It keeps happening. Just like with the tense conflict. &quot;She always wondered&#8230; but then thought to herself&#8230;&quot; You seem pretty intent to beat it into my head that she's thinking, telling me she wondered, then thought, then stopped thinking, because that's easy to do when you've got a nagging concern. Here's a better way of saying that:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>She wondered where the Foundation got such nasty ailments, but quickly decided she didn't want to know.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>She continued to work uninterrupted until her desk phone began to ring.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Paraphrasing: She was uninterrupted until something interrupted her. Better way of phrasing:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>She continued to work until interrupted by a call to her office.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>The dry lady's tone was replaced with the dead line sound, and Amy put her phone back down before standing up.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's funny how word placement can completely change the meaning of a sentence. This sentence tells us a few things:</p>
<ul>
<li>The lady had probably not just gotten out of the shower.</li>
<li>The roof is probably intact, or at the very least, it isn't presently raining where she is.</li>
<li>There are probably no major water line breaks in the room.</li>
<li>She has not spilled any beverage or other fluid on herself.</li>
<li>She may be a generally boring person.</li>
<li>She may be dehydrated.</li>
<li>She is probably not particularly aroused.</li>
</ul>
<p>However, while these things may be true, they're probably not what you meant. I'm unsure why she picked up the entire phone and had to set it back down, rather than just had to hang it up. I've also seen no indication she was in some way physically impaired and would have a means to relocate without standing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>She really hated when people interrupted her from her work. She found herself most at-ease when working.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a big problem of yours. You keep telling us what everyone thinks and feels, rather than showing.</p>
<p>This post is reaching absurd length so I'm going to stop here.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

							</div>

								
					
